# S begins as a number and is later given one literal. What is proven of it is `undefined|number|L0`; what is expected of it is a number, for so it begins. A name read off a number has no typed form, so every label of the switch was a
# closed read of a value of which not even "it is a cell" was known: half the run of a parser that keeps its states so. Where nothing at all was proven of S and the literal merely expected, the same read was one operation: a proof gave
# dearer code than no proof. Of what is proven to be layouts or primitives, a read by name expects the layouts: one test of the cell, the slot behind it, the closed read as the other way.
# f1 = fill, f2 = told
# RUN(READ): walk f2 mechanisms=all
# REQUIRES(READ): as-written
# READ: GetSlot {{*}} key="TEXT" receivers=[L0]
# RUN(ANSWERS): run-in-shell globalThis.sink = () => {};
